  "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOrchestrators (Kubernetes, Fly, Render, container platforms) have no probe that answers \"has the Gateway finished starting?\" without also folding in downstream channel health. `/readyz` flips to 503 when any channel account hard-fails (`blocked`, `terminal-disconnect`, post-grace `not-running` — `src/gateway/server/readiness.ts`), so one broken Telegram account takes an otherwise healthy Gateway and Control UI out of traffic rotation or blocks a rollout. There is also no HTTP surface exposing the running version, which makes \"which build is this deployment actually running?\" needlessly hard to answer for cloud operators.\n\nThe bundled deployment templates compound this:\n\n- `fly.toml` declared no health checks at all.\n- `render.yaml` used `/health` (pure liveness) as Render's traffic-admission signal.\n- The Kubernetes manifest combined the mutable `:slim` tag with `imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent` (upgrades silently never happen) and an init container that unconditionally overwrote the persisted `openclaw.json` and workspace `AGENTS.md` on every pod start, erasing product-made edits.\n- `docs/install/fly.md` still claimed the image `HEALTHCHECK` is pinned to port 18789; stale since `bc4221a07e9` (#116639) made `docker-healthcheck` resolve the active Gateway lock port.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nFirst step of the cloud-container deployment track: give every host one correct, additive probe contract before building anything platform-specific. `/readyz` semantics are a shipped public contract (existing manifests probe it), so this adds a probe rather than changing one.\n\n- New `/startup` + `/startupz` routes: `200 {\"ok\":true,\"status\":\"started\"}` once startup work is complete and the Gateway is not draining; `503` with `status:\"starting\"` (plus `pendingReason` for privileged callers) during startup; `503` with `status:\"draining\"` during drain. Channel health is never consulted.\n- The startup policy is one factory (`createStartupChecker`); the readiness checker now composes it instead of duplicating the startup/draining branches.\n- Version (`resolveRuntimeServiceVersion`) and `uptimeMs` are included only for local-direct or authenticated callers, reusing the exact detail-gating already applied to `/readyz` subsystem names (now a shared helper). Remote unauthenticated probes get `{ok,status}` only — no build fingerprinting surface.\n- Templates now use `/startupz` for startup/traffic admission, keep `/healthz` for liveness, and leave `/readyz` for channel-aware monitoring. K8s image pinned to the immutable `2026.7.1-2-slim` tag; init container seeds config only when missing.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- A failed channel account no longer evicts a healthy Gateway/Control UI from Service endpoints or fails platform health checks on the bundled templates.\n- Operators and supervisors can read the running Gateway version over HTTP with their existing token (or locally) — no WebSocket handshake needed.\n- Kubernetes deployments stop silently ignoring upgrades and stop losing config edits on pod restart.\n- No behavior change to `/healthz`, `/readyz`, or any existing response shape. No new config or env surface.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Regression proof: the new assertions fail on pre-change code (`node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-http.probe.test.ts src/gateway/server.plugin-http-auth.test.ts` → 6 failed pre-fix, all for the missing routes/factory).\n- Post-fix: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-http.probe.test.ts src/gateway/server.plugin-http-auth.test.ts src/gateway/server/readiness.test.ts` green (output in PR checks / see below).\n- New coverage: startup lifecycle transitions (starting → started → draining), hard channel failure flips `/readyz` but not `/startupz`, detail gating (remote unauthenticated gets minimal body; authenticated gets version/uptime/pendingReason), GET/HEAD parity with Content-Length, 404 on suffixed probe paths.\n- K8s image tag verified against Docker Hub's exact-tag API (active amd64/arm64 manifest) and the `<release-tag>-slim` publication rule in `.github/workflows/docker-release.yml`.\n- Docs updated: `docs/gateway/health.md` (probe table + semantics), `docs/install/{docker,fly,kubernetes,render}` including the corrected Fly healthcheck-port explanation.\n",
